# SQL injection via a template-literal query: a query sink (execute/query/
# prepare) called with a backtick string that contains SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/
# DELETE AND a ${...} interpolation. Template-literal interpolation of user
# input into SQL is the same defect as string concatenation (CWE-89), but the
# concat rule (KEUR-SQLI-001) only matches quote-delimited strings, so this
# backtick form was a false negative (found via mestjs authored ground truth,
# MEST-SQLITE-001, 2026-09-26).
#
# A parameterised query has no ${...} inside the SQL string, so it does not
# match — the ${...} is the discriminator between safe and unsafe.
id: KEUR-SQLI-002
maturity: 1
title: Potential SQL injection via template-literal interpolation
category: sqli
severity: critical
engine: pattern
action: block
# skip_comments intentionally OFF: keur's skip_comments blanks matched
# code on .ts/.js (verified 2026-09-26, same bug that killed SEC-038).
target:
  extensions: .js .ts
patterns:
  - regex: (?i)\b(?:execute|query|prepare|raw|run|all|get|exec)\s*\(\s*`[^`]*\b(?:select|insert|update|delete|drop|union|where|from)\b[^`]*\$\{[^}]+\}
    message: A SQL query sink is called with a template literal that interpolates a value (${...}) directly into the SQL — potential SQL injection (CWE-89). Use parameterised queries with bound placeholders (?, $1) instead of template interpolation.
confidence: 0.8
fix: Use parameterised queries or an ORM with bound parameters. Replace `db.prepare(`... ${x}`)` with `db.prepare('... ?').get(x)`.
